2026-06-08 23:29:57 - INFO - Processing batch no: 450 | item no: 38
2026-06-08 23:29:57 - INFO - Generating symptom summary | metadata={'content': '- Most  newborns  cry  immediately  at  birth  and  therefore  need no  assistance  to  establish breathing.  If  the  baby  is  preterm,  meconium stained or some evidence of infection, not crying or breathing or with poor muscle tone, then proceed to initial steps of resuscitation.\n- For  term  babies,  use  100%  oxygen  when  baby  is  cyanotic  or  when  positive  pressure ventilation  is  required.  If  room  air  is  being  used,  supplementary  oxygen  should  be available  to  use  if  there  is  no  appreciable  improvement  within  90  seconds  of  birth.  If supplemental oxygen is not available, positive pressure ventilation should be continued with room air.\n- Infants without signs of life (no heart beat and no respiratory effort) after 10 minutes of resuscitation show either a high mortality or severe neurodevelopmental disability. After 10 minutes of continuous and adequate resuscitative efforts, discontinuation of resuscitation may be justified if there are no signs of life.', 'disease_category': 'SURGERY', 'disease_name': 'Routine care of newborn (regime/therapy)'}
